WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Most people who've had chickenpox don't catch it

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:03.120
again.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.840
Years later, they can breathe in the same

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.320
virus and usually don't get sick.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.880
You might think that's because antibodies from the

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.040
first time are still in their blood.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.680
But by then, the antibody level has usually

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.000
fallen far below its peak.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.160
So what's stopping the virus?

00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:23.286
Let's go back to the first infection.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:25.846
As you saw in the last two videos,

00:00:25.927 --> 00:00:28.086
only a few B cells and T cells

00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:29.606
fit the virus at first.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:32.086
They have to be selected and divide for

00:00:32.247 --> 00:00:35.686
days, while innate defences hold the virus back.

00:00:36.007 --> 00:00:38.246
So antibodies only appear in the blood a

00:00:38.407 --> 00:00:40.806
week or more after infection, and they peak

00:00:40.887 --> 00:00:41.366
later.

00:00:42.007 --> 00:00:45.046
Meanwhile the virus spreads, and you get sick.

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:48.646
This first reaction is the primary response.

00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:51.826
Then the infection is cleared.

00:00:52.107 --> 00:00:54.626
Most plasma cells die within weeks, so the

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:56.386
antibody level falls.

00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:59.866
But the clones also made memory cells, memory

00:00:59.947 --> 00:01:02.586
B cells and memory T cells.

00:01:02.707 --> 00:01:04.826
These are long-lived and stay in the body

00:01:04.947 --> 00:01:07.546
for years, many for decades.

00:01:07.627 --> 00:01:09.466
And there are now far more cells that

00:01:09.547 --> 00:01:10.906
fit this virus than there were

00:01:11.427 --> 00:01:11.999
before.

00:01:12.054 --> 00:01:14.746
So the memory is stored mainly in cells,

00:01:14.827 --> 00:01:16.346
not in leftover antibody.

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:19.806
Now the same virus arrives again.

00:01:20.207 --> 00:01:23.566
This time, many fitting cells are already waiting,

00:01:23.647 --> 00:01:26.046
so the virus's antigens meet them quickly.

00:01:26.527 --> 00:01:29.166
Memory B cells need fewer divisions, and they

00:01:29.247 --> 00:01:31.086
turn into plasma cells fast.

00:01:31.647 --> 00:01:34.526
Memory helper T cells give the go-ahead quickly,

00:01:34.607 --> 00:01:37.886
and memory cytotoxic T cells destroy infected cells

00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:38.366
sooner.

00:01:38.927 --> 00:01:42.526
So antibodies rise within days, climb far higher,

00:01:42.607 --> 00:01:43.726
and stay up longer.

00:01:44.287 --> 00:01:46.766
This is the secondary response, and it often

00:01:46.847 --> 00:01:49.006
clears the virus before you feel sick.

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:53.193
On a graph of antibody level against time,

00:01:53.274 --> 00:01:56.793
the primary response is a slow, low hump.

00:01:56.834 --> 00:02:00.473
The secondary response is a fast, tall peak.

00:02:00.514 --> 00:02:03.033
The scale is often logarithmic, because the second

00:02:03.114 --> 00:02:05.193
peak can be ten to a hundred times

00:02:05.274 --> 00:02:05.993
higher.

00:02:06.314 --> 00:02:09.273
But this only happens for the same antigen.

00:02:09.314 --> 00:02:11.353
If a different antigen arrives at the same

00:02:11.554 --> 00:02:14.553
time, it gets a primary response, slow and

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:17.113
low, because no memory cells fit it.

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:23.133
So remember: second exposure, faster, bigger, longer.

00:02:23.374 --> 00:02:26.413
It starts sooner, makes much more antibody and

00:02:26.614 --> 00:02:29.773
lasts longer, because memory cells are already there.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.600
Vaccination uses this.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.160
A vaccine gives you the antigen in a

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.480
form that doesn't cause the full disease.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.360
It might be a weakened live virus, like

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.640
the chickenpox vaccine, a killed pathogen, just one

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.520
of its proteins, or instructions that let your

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.200
cells make that protein.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.640
Your body makes a primary response and memory

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.480
cells, without the illness.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.560
Then a real infection meets a secondary response.

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:01.726
Some vaccines need an extra dose later, called

00:03:01.807 --> 00:03:02.526
a booster.

00:03:03.007 --> 00:03:05.966
It's another exposure, so it triggers a secondary

00:03:06.247 --> 00:03:09.006
response, which raises the antibody level and adds

00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:10.606
more memory cells.

00:03:10.927 --> 00:03:13.806
And a vaccine prepares you before an infection.

00:03:14.207 --> 00:03:16.686
It doesn't cure an illness you already have.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.260
Immunity from vaccines and infections is called active

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.980
immunity, because your own immune system did the

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.940
work and made memory cells.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.180
It often lasts a long time, and a

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.220
booster can top it up, but it takes

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.580
a week or more to build.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.260
Catching the disease gives natural active immunity.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.700
A vaccine gives artificial active immunity.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.740
Passive immunity is different.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.020
You're given antibodies that another person or animal

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:45.500
made.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.700
They work straight away, but receiving them doesn't

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.660
itself make your B cells form memory cells.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.540
The antibodies are broken down over weeks to

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.540
months, and protection fades.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.900
A baby gets natural passive immunity from its

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:03.020
mother's antibodies, across the placenta and in breast

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:03.420
milk.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.060
That protects it for its first months, until

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.340
it can build its own.

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:12.446
Artificial passive immunity is an injection of antibodies.

00:04:12.727 --> 00:04:15.606
Antivenom for a snakebite is the classic example.

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:18.006
It's made by giving an animal, often a

00:04:18.087 --> 00:04:21.046
horse, small doses of venom and collecting the

00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:22.326
antibodies it makes.

00:04:22.887 --> 00:04:26.006
A bite victim needs those antibodies now, because

00:04:26.127 --> 00:04:28.486
their own primary response would take far too

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:29.046
long.

00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:34.186
Active and passive immunity are both acquired immunity,

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:37.306
gained during your life, unlike the innate defences

00:04:37.387 --> 00:04:38.186
you're born with.

00:04:38.747 --> 00:04:40.706
Put it together with two questions.

00:04:40.987 --> 00:04:44.266
Who made the antibodies, you or something else?

00:04:44.507 --> 00:04:47.546
And how did it happen, naturally, or by

00:04:47.627 --> 00:04:48.506
medical treatment?

00:04:49.147 --> 00:04:50.986
Active, you do the work.

00:04:51.387 --> 00:04:53.946
Passive, you're handed the antibodies.

00:04:54.267 --> 00:04:58.106
So a snakebite needs ready-made antibodies now, while

00:04:58.187 --> 00:05:01.146
lasting protection against a disease comes from active

00:05:01.227 --> 00:05:03.146
immunity, such as a vaccine.

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:05.806
Two exam questions.

00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:06.846
First.

00:05:07.487 --> 00:05:09.966
The graph shows antibody levels in the blood

00:05:10.047 --> 00:05:10.966
of a rabbit.

00:05:11.247 --> 00:05:13.886
On day zero, it was injected with antigen

00:05:14.047 --> 00:05:14.446
P.

00:05:15.087 --> 00:05:17.646
On day forty, it was injected with antigen

00:05:17.727 --> 00:05:21.006
P again, along with a new antigen, Q.

00:05:21.647 --> 00:05:24.366
Explain the difference between the responses to P

00:05:24.527 --> 00:05:27.166
and to Q after day forty.

00:05:27.247 --> 00:05:28.526
Pause here to answer.

00:05:38.934 --> 00:05:42.373
After day forty, antibodies to P rose within

00:05:42.454 --> 00:05:44.773
days to a much higher peak.

00:05:44.854 --> 00:05:46.933
That's a secondary response.

00:05:47.014 --> 00:05:49.493
The first injection left memory cells that fit

00:05:49.654 --> 00:05:52.213
P, so many cells were ready.

00:05:52.294 --> 00:05:55.573
Q got a slow, low primary response, because

00:05:55.654 --> 00:05:58.133
no memory cells fit Q, and its few

00:05:58.214 --> 00:06:00.373
fitting B cells had to be selected and

00:06:00.454 --> 00:06:01.853
multiply first.

00:06:02.134 --> 00:06:05.333
Thinking the first injection boosts every antibody is

00:06:05.374 --> 00:06:06.773
the classic mistake.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:08.960
Second.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.600
A bushwalker is bitten by a brown snake,

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:14.080
given antivenom, and recovers.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.640
A year later, she's bitten again.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:18.960
Her friend says she's now immune, because she's

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.680
already had antibodies against the venom.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:23.440
Evaluate the friend's claim.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.040
Pause here to answer.

00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:37.066
The claim is wrong.

00:06:37.307 --> 00:06:40.266
Antivenom gives artificial passive immunity.

00:06:40.507 --> 00:06:43.906
It supplied horse antibodies, which fade within weeks.

00:06:44.187 --> 00:06:46.506
Receiving them doesn't make her own B cells

00:06:46.587 --> 00:06:48.106
form memory cells.

00:06:48.187 --> 00:06:50.426
So it gave her no dependable protection against

00:06:50.507 --> 00:06:53.386
another bite, and she'd need antivenom again.

00:06:53.707 --> 00:06:56.986
Calling antivenom a vaccine is the classic mistake.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.560
So for any immunity question, ask two things.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.440
Are there memory cells for this antigen?

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.440
And who made the antibodies?

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:11.240
Then decide: primary or secondary, active or passive.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.280
Next, practice questions that put the whole immune

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:15.480
response together.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.140
Here are the key notes.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.300
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:21.180
to copy them.
